the Irish border was at the heart of 30  years of violence  now the checkpoints and fortified  British army bases have gone the  troubles have ended and people and goods  flow across the border but brexit  threatens this progress why the open  border between Northern Ireland and the  Republic of Ireland allows people to  identify as British or Irish or both but  during a 30-year conflict known as the  troubles the British Army fortified and  closed the border to stop terrorist  groups like the IRA moving between the  two countries but communities were  separated sectarian violence between  Catholic and Protestants continued and  the checkpoints and army bases became a  symbol for those opposed to British rule  the checkpoints were dismantled after  the Good Friday Agreement was signed the  open border allows people and goods to  move freely across the island of Ireland  but unless a deal is struck bricks it  could lead to the return of a hard  border as immigration and customs checks  would need to take place between the two  countries that's because after brexit  the United Kingdom will be out of the  European Union the EU will have a new  external border where Northern Ireland  meets the Republic of Ireland the border  is 310 miles long weaving through  countryside lakes rivers and farmland  there are 208 crossings where every  month over 170,000 lorries and 200,000  vans travel between the two countries  every day around 30,000 people cross the  border to get to work so someone needs  to figure out how to keep things moving  this is where the backstop comes in  Baxter Baxter backstop backstop the back  the backstop is simply a back-up plan  for politicians British Irish any you  figure out what to do next  if the EU and UK can't reach a new  training arrangement the UK as a whole  remains in the customs union with the EU  while Northern Ireland would follow  further EU rules and regulations to keep  the Irish border frictionless but some  argue this is risky because it would  effectively trap a UK and a customs  union with the EU which wasn't the goal  of people who voted to leave an  alternative arrangement proposed  originally by the EU is a backstop  applying to Northern Ireland only to  keep the border open and trade flowing  that would mean avoiding controls and  checks on the island of Ireland because  the customs border would be here but  there is opposition to this idea - as  Northern Ireland would be treated  differently to the rest of the UK which  could be free to strike trade bill
 

